.TH ESDM\-SERVER 8 "2026-10-09" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-server \- Entropy Source and DRNG Manager daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-server
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-server
is the central daemon of the Entropy Source and DRNG Manager (ESDM). It
collects data from all compiled\-in entropy sources, maintains the
deterministic random number generators seeded from them, and serves random
data to consumers.
.PP
Consumers reach the daemon through one of its frontends:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the RPC interface used by
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR esdm\-tool (1)
and the other ESDM frontends,
.IP \(bu 2
the character devices
.I /dev/random
and
.I /dev/urandom
provided by
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random (8)
and
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom (8),
.IP \(bu 2
the EGD protocol, if a socket is configured with
.B \-\-egd_socket
(see
.BR OPTIONS ).
.RE
.PP
The daemon must be started as root. It creates its listening sockets and its
status shared memory segment while privileged and then permanently drops to an
unprivileged user; see
.B \-\-username
and
.BR \-\-groupname .
Entropy sources that need privileges for their setup are initialized before
the privileges are dropped.
.PP
On systemd installations the daemon is normally not started by hand but
through the shipped
.B esdm\-server.service
unit and its socket units.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version of the ESDM library the daemon was built against and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Increase the logging verbosity. May be given repeatedly. Any use of this
option implies
.BR \-\-foreground .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the PID of the daemon to
.IR FILE .
The daemon keeps the file locked for as long as it runs, its shutdown included,
so another instance cannot start before it is gone. It writes the file
before it drops its privileges, and can then no longer remove it from a
directory only root may write to, such as
.IR /run .
So at shutdown it empties the file instead, which then names no process; the
next start reuses it, and systemd removes the file of a service configured with
.B PIDFile=
itself. With
.BR \-\-pid_namespace ,
the file names the supervisor, which keeps its privileges and removes the file
when it exits.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-username " \fIUSER\fR"
Unprivileged user to switch to after the privileged initialization. Defaults
to
.BR nobody .
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-groupname " \fIGROUP\fR"
Supplemental group to switch to after the privileged initialization. By
default no supplemental group is set.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Do not detach from the terminal.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-force_irqes
Force the interrupt entropy source to be enabled in cases where the ESDM
would otherwise keep retrying to enable it.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-force_schedes
The same for the scheduler entropy source.
.TP
.B \-\-jent_block_disable
Disable the asynchronous block collection of the Jitter RNG entropy source.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-syslog
Log to syslog instead of standard output and standard error.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-raise_sched_priority
Raise the scheduling priority (lower the nice level) of the daemon.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-memlock
Lock all memory of the daemon so that it is never swapped out, and disable
core dumps. Keeping seed material out of swap and out of crash dumps is the
point of this option.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-small_memory
Reduce the memory footprint: 1 MiB thread stacks and a single DRNG node
instead of one per CPU node.
.TP
.B \-\-keep_ipc
Do not remove the RPC sockets, the status shared memory segment and the
semaphores on shutdown. This lets running CUSE clients survive a restart of
the daemon.
.TP
.B \-\-pid_namespace
Fork the daemon into an isolating PID namespace. The starting process remains
as its supervisor.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-egd_socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Additionally serve the EGD protocol on the UNIX domain socket
.IR PATH .
Disabled by default.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-egd_socket_pr " \fIPATH\fR"
Serve the EGD protocol backed by the prediction\-resistance generator on
.IR PATH .
The EGD protocol cannot select the generator per request, hence the separate
socket.
.TP
.BR \-\-pkcs11_module " \fIPATH\fR"
Load the PKCS#11 provider module
.I PATH
for the PKCS#11 entropy source instead of the module the build was configured
with (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11_module_path ).
The module is loaded while the daemon still has its privileges. Refused by a
build without the PKCS#11 entropy source (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11 ).
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_lib " \fIPATH\fR"
Load the plugin library
.I PATH
as external entropy source instead of the one the build was configured with
(meson option
.BR es_ext_lib_path ),
see
.B EXTERNAL ENTROPY SOURCES
below.
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_lib_entropy_rate " \fIBITS\fR"
Credit a block of the plugin with at most
.I BITS
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256, instead of the rate of the
build (meson option
.BR es_ext_lib_entropy_rate ,
256 by default).
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket " \fIPATH\fR"
Read the external entropy source a provider process serves on the UNIX domain
socket
.I PATH
instead of the one the build was configured with (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_path ),
see
.B EXTERNAL ENTROPY SOURCES
below.
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate " \fIBITS\fR"
Credit a block of the provider with at most
.I BITS
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256 (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_entropy_rate ).
The default is 0: the data of a provider is not credited with entropy until a
rate is configured.
.TP
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_uid " \fIUSER\fR"
Accept a provider running as
.I USER
\- a uid or a user name \- next to one running as root (meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_peer_uid ).
.SH EXTERNAL ENTROPY SOURCES
Noise sources that are not part of the ESDM are written against
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)
as a plugin and handed to the daemon in one of two ways.
.PP
A plugin library given with
.B \-\-es_ext_lib
is loaded into the daemon and its initialization callback run while the daemon
still has its privileges \- a device the plugin reads is opened there.
Afterwards the plugin runs with the privileges and in the namespaces of the
daemon. A library that cannot be loaded, exports no plugin of the supported
version, or fails its initialization leaves the source switched off; the
reason is logged.
.PP
Each block is credited with the entropy the plugin claims for it, but with at
most what the configured rate allows. A plugin that fails to deliver, or claims
more entropy than the block can hold, is credited with nothing until it
delivers again; the daemon asks it again on its own, at most every 5 seconds,
to find out. A plugin failing its self test is credited with nothing until a
self test passes.
.PP
A provider given with
.B \-\-es_ext_socket
is a process of its own serving the source with the provider server of
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3),
such as
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8).
The daemon connects to it at start up and whenever the connection was lost,
at most every 5 seconds after a failed attempt, and sends one request at a time.
It checks the credentials of the provider on every connection: anybody who can
create the socket file could otherwise feed the daemon with data credited as
entropy, so only a provider running as root, or as the user given with
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_uid ,
is accepted. The daemon connects with its privileges dropped once a connection
was lost, so the socket has to let the user it runs as
.RB ( \-\-username )
connect. A provider that does not answer within 2 seconds, or answers what the
protocol does not allow, is disconnected and not connected to again for 5
seconds; one that is not connected, or failed, is credited with nothing until
it delivers again, which the daemon finds out on its own. The same entropy credit
applies as for a plugin, with the rate of
.BR \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT ", " SIGQUIT ", " SIGHUP
Shut the daemon down cleanly.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Mark the ESDM as suspended in the status shared memory segment, so that
consumers waiting on it notice that the daemon is about to be suspended. This
is what
.BR esdm\-server\-signal\-helper (8)
sends before the system goes to sleep.
.SH CONNECTION LIMITS
All interfaces share the file descriptor limit of the daemon (the shipped
.B esdm\-server.service
sets
.BR LimitNOFILE=8192 ).
So that no single local user can exhaust it and lock every other consumer out,
the daemon bounds the connections of each unprivileged user. Processes running
as root are exempt. A user is not simply the UID of the connecting process: an
unprivileged user may map the UIDs of its subordinate range in a user
namespace and connect as each of them, and these count as the user's own. A
process in a user namespace other than the daemon's is counted as the owner of
the outermost user namespace between the two that is not owned by root, as far
as the kernel shows the daemon that namespace \- which, once the daemon
dropped its privileges, it does only for processes it may trace. Otherwise, a
UID within a range of
.I /etc/subuid
counts as the user owning the range, unless that is root or does not resolve
to a UID; changes of the file take effect within a second. Any other UID is a
user of its own, and so is each UID of a user namespace that root created and
filled itself, such as that of a container.
.TP
.B RPC interface
At most 16 concurrent connections per online CPU and user, counted over all
RPC connections of all server threads - a client process holds up to one
connection per online CPU. The bound is at least 512 and at most half of the
file descriptor limit of the daemon; it is logged at startup. A connection that has been idle for the period set by the
.B esdm\-server\-rpc\-idle\-timeout\-usec
build option (2 seconds by default) is closed;
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
reconnects transparently.
The unprivileged connections of all UIDs together leave 2560 descriptors, but
at most half of the limit, to the EGD and privileged interfaces. This bound is
split evenly over the server threads, at most 1024 connections each, and logged
at startup. A thread whose share is taken leaves new connections to the others.
Once all shares are taken, a thread does not refuse a new connection but
closes one of its idle ones \- the least recently used one of the user holding
the most connections in that thread. A connection with a request not read yet
is never closed for that, nor is one of the privileged interface. One with an
answer its client has not read yet is closed only when no idle one is left; the
client still reads that answer.
.TP
.B EGD interface
Served by one worker thread per online CPU, at most four, each serving the
clients it accepted. At most 2048 concurrent connections in total and 256 per
user. A connection
that has received no command and owes no response for 60 seconds is closed
only when its slot is wanted: at most 256 slots are left, an accept ran out of
descriptors within that time, or its user was refused a connection and is still
at its limit. While slots are short, only the idle connections of the users
holding the most connections are closed, never those of root. Once all slots
are taken, a new
connection is not left waiting but a connection is closed to make room for it
\- one of a user holding the most connections of all, preferably of the user
of the new connection, the least recently active one with nothing owed to its
client if there is one, and never one of root or of a user holding fewer. A
server thread that serves no connection of such a user leaves the new
connection to the others, or turns it away.
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
reconnects transparently, which a client confined by seccomp after it connected
may not survive.
A worker thread spends at most 25 ms per round of its event loop on generating
prediction resistant data, one generation per connection, so that the clients
of the prediction resistant socket cannot hold up the others of that thread;
a request beyond that waits for the next round, which follows at once.
.PP
A connection beyond the limit of its user is accepted and closed at once. The
daemon logs a warning when a user reaches its limit, and again only after the
user has dropped below its limit in between. Apart from the scaling of the RPC bound, these limits are compile\-time
constants.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-rpc\-unpriv.socket
Socket of the unprivileged RPC interface. Its directory is set by the
.B esdm\-server\-rpc\-path\-unprivileged
build option.
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-rpc\-priv.socket
Socket of the privileged RPC interface. Its directory is set by the
.B esdm\-server\-rpc\-path\-privileged
build option.
.TP
.I /run/esdm\-egd.socket
Default socket of the EGD interface, when the shipped systemd socket unit is
used.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B esdm\-server
exits with a non\-zero status when it cannot initialize the ESDM or the
requested interfaces.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-tool (1),
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random (8),
.BR esdm\-cuse\-urandom (8),
.BR esdm\-proc (8),
.BR esdm\-kernel\-seeder (8),
.BR esdm\-server\-signal\-helper (8),
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
